<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Joshua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Joshua is the sixth book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, describing the Israelite conquest and division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>' successor Joshua. Its accounts of the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, Ai and Hazor were long read as history, but archaeology shows a more complex picture, and most scholars regard the book as composed centuries after the events it describes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-joshua.jpg" alt="Book of Joshua" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papyrus_with_the_fragments_of_the_Book_of_Joshua_(CBL_Cpt_2019.8a).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The book is anonymous. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Babylonian Talmud</a> attributed it to Joshua himself, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> rejected that view, and by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a> it was recognised that the book was written much later than the period it depicts. Scholars now agree that it was composed as part of the Deuteronomistic history, which runs from Deuteronomy to the Books of Kings, first at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> in the late 7th century BCE and extensively revised in the 6th. The earliest parts are probably chapters 2 to 11, the conquest story. The book was not completed until after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> in 586 BCE.</p><p>Fragments of Joshua from the Hasmonean period were found among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a> at Qumran, and the earliest complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> copy is in the Aleppo Codex of the 10th century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book tells of Israelite campaigns in central, southern and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and the division of the land among the Twelve Tribes. It opens after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, with God's commission to Joshua, and ends with Joshua's farewell address and the covenant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>. The conquest runs from the reconnaissance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> and the crossing of the Jordan to the fall of Jericho, Ai, and campaigns in the south and north. The land is the central subject, and obedience to the Law and to the command of herem, the ban on treaties, mercy and intermarriage with the enemy, is a constant theme.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The prevailing scholarly view is that Joshua is not a factual account. Archaeology indicates a gradual, indigenous emergence of Israel from Canaanite society. The 13th-century BCE setting coincides with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Bronze Age Collapse</a>, but with few exceptions, Hazor and Lachish among them, the cities destroyed are not those the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> associates with Joshua, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> and Ai show little sign of occupation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, though recent excavations at Jericho have questioned this. In the 1930s Martin Noth criticised the book's usefulness for history.</p><p>Many scholars read the book as describing what would now be called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and the archaeologist William G. Dever uses that word. Others hold that the label is anachronistic. They explain the killings by cultural norms, since Israel was not the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> state to practise herem, and by theology, and some take the violent language as hyperbole, comparable to extrabiblical military reports.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Book of Joshua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scribes at the courts of Hezekiah and Josiah are widely believed to have collected and edited earlier traditions, and the Deuteronomistic History, the account running from <b>Joshua</b> to Kings, interprets Israel’s past in light of Josiah’s reform and the later disaster of exile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Book of Joshua</b> credits its destruction to the Israelites; scholars still debate who was responsible.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hazor">Kingdom of Hazor</a></li></ul></section>
